WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.000
1. Choose Advanced and SquareIceShelf
Select Cloud → Advanced → ISSM → SquareIceShelf.
This diagnostic experiment changes the upstream thickness parameter while retaining the native SSA setup.
The application example remains read-only until cloned.

2
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:29.000
2. Clone to an editable workspace
Enter a new name and choose Clone to My Workspace.
The recorded confirmation creates pilot-issm-advanced-1119, retaining the native example files.
This owned clone is separate from the canonical application example.

3
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:47.000
3. Import the changed parameter file
Prepare Square.par with only hmax changed from 1000 to 1010 m. Choose script files, select Square.par, enable Replace existing script files, then Import script files.
The recorded confirmation says Square.par is saved.
The GUI import is shown. Inline editing of the parameter was not completed.

4
00:00:47.000 --> 00:01:05.000
4. Interpret the scientific change
hmin stays 300 m. The +10 m change at hmax tapers along the y-dependent thickness profile; it is not a uniform +10 m addition.
Keep runme.m as the Run target. Its canonical SSA setup requests two MPI processes.
All four submitted example-file hashes match the expected source; only Square.par carries the intended parameter change.

5
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:20.000
5. Review the native cloud run
Review & Launch identifies ISSM and the owned Advanced example.
The job used 2 vCPU / 8 GiB with the existing licensed MATLAB workflow, then succeeded with exit code 0.
The displayed ~60 min estimate is separate from actual model timing.

6
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:36.000
6. Select the accepted native fields
In Results, choose StressbalanceSolution and Vel.
This accepted Advanced run contains 91 mesh vertices and 148 triangles. Vx, Vy, Vel and Pressure are finite at all 91 nodes.
Result identity is bound to this run’s downloaded archive; the launch-form selector is excluded.

7
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:54.000
7. Inspect the full saved velocity figure
This is the unchanged native PNG from the accepted Advanced archive, with its full title, axes and colour bar.
Speed spans 0–9272.778005 m/year. The x and y axes are metres.
The plotted Vel values agree with hypot(Vx, Vy) in the exported numeric fields.

8
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:09.000
8. Read the convergence count correctly
The saved StressbalanceConvergenceNumSteps scalar is 23.
This is the diagnostic SSA stress-balance convergence count. The run does not have a transient time axis.
The other four saved fields are nodal arrays; the convergence count is a single scalar.

9
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:24.000
9. Keep the changed-case comparison scoped
The exported mesh exactly matches the accepted Basic mesh.
Advanced maximum speed is 9272.778005 m/year; Basic was 9034.387288 m/year. These are descriptive results for different thickness inputs.
No repeat-equality or accuracy claim is made. The hmax/hmin values are source-confirmed; the opaque saved MATLAB model was not decoded.

10
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:39.000
10. Separate readiness from actual runtime
The native job succeeded with exit code 0. Submission → Batch start took 183.642 s; the Batch task took 36.831 s.
Runtime provenance records MATLAB R2024b Update 7 and ISSM 2026.1.
The unchanged runme.m requests two MPI processes. Setup/editing waits are omitted from this tutorial.

11
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:53.000
11. Save the current Advanced archive
Download results produced this run’s fresh HTTPS link. The browser saved an 11-member ZIP with valid CRC.
It contains the mesh, numeric fields, native PNG, final MATLAB model and runtime/RNG provenance.
The ZIP hash and all member hashes are retained in the reproducibility receipt.
